---
permalink: /sitemap.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  The URL list a search engine reads. Generated from collections.all rather than typed, on the
  same principle as the published-figure counts on /sources-and-method/: a hand-kept list of
  this site's own pages is a second home for something the build already knows, and it goes
  stale the first time a page is added.

  404.html is not here because it excludes itself from collections, which is correct twice
  over: a sitemap lists pages a reader should be able to find.

  The five Nunjucks pages carried eleventyExcludeFromCollections until this file existed, and
  it has been removed from them. Nothing else in the repository reads collections.all, and the
  claims collection is a glob over content/claims, so the flag was doing nothing except hiding
  the overview, the three theme pages and the claims index from the only list that needed them.
  A page that carries it again drops out of the sitemap in silence, so check-build.mjs compares
  this file against the pages the build wrote, in both directions, and names the flag.

  No <lastmod>, deliberately. The only per-page date this site holds is last_reviewed, which
  records what a review READ, not when the file changed, and publishing it as a modification
  date would assert something the site does not know. A sitemap with no lastmod is valid and
  is still a complete list of URLs, which is the part Search Console uses.

  robots.txt points at this file, in the `Sitemap:` line the launch wrote under call 26, and the
  wildcard group admits crawlers, so this file is what a crawler is sent to first. It said the
  opposite until 11 August 2026, that "robots.txt disallows everything and this file is inert",
  which the launch changeset made false without touching this file: the gate it named as future
  work is the change that shipped beside it.

  Sorted by url, which a sitemap does not require and this project does. Eleventy orders
  collections.all by date, and a page with no date in its front matter takes its file's mtime, so
  a fresh clone emits the same URLs in whatever order the checkout happened to write them. This
  said "the same 16 URLs" until 11 August 2026, when the build made rather more; the count is
  what `npm run build` prints and does not belong in a comment. The
  first build of this file in a clean worktree produced a diff of eleven moved lines against a
  build of the same commit. Nothing was wrong, and that is the problem: proving a change by
  diffing two builds is how this project verifies almost everything, and an artefact that reorders
  itself puts noise in the one signal it relies on. check-build.mjs compares as sets and was never
  affected either way.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for item in collections.all | sort(false, false, "url") %}
{%- if item.outputPath and item.outputPath.endsWith(".html") %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ item.url }}</loc></url>
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
